We consider the Cauchy problem for the defocusing Hirota equation with non-zero boundary conditions \begin{align*} &iq_t+ q_{xx}-2\left(|q|^2-1\right)q +i\left(q_{xxx}-6|q|^2q_x\right)=0,\\ &q(x,0)=q_0(x)\to\pm 1, \quad x\to\pm\infty, \end{align*} which can be characterized using a Riemann-Hilbert problem through the inverse scattering transform. With the $\bar{\partial}$-steepest descent method and double scaling limit technique, we derive the long-time asymptotics of the solution in two transition regions \begin{align*}
  \mathcal{P}_{a}
  :=\left\{(x,t)\in\mathbb{R}\times\mathbb{R}^{+}:
  \left| {x}/{t}-a\right|t^{2/3}<C\right\},
  \quad a\in\{-4,-8\}, \end{align*} where $C>0$ is a constant. It is shown that the asymptotic expansions in two transition regions can be expressed in terms of the Hastings--McLeod solution of the Painlev\'e II equation in the generic case and Ablowitz--Segur solutions in the non-generic case.

For an integer $n > 1$, let $P^+(n)$ be the largest prime factor of $n$. We show that a positive proportion of integers $n\leq x$ satisfy\begin{align*}
  P^+(n-1)<P^+(n)>P^+(n+1)<P^+(n+2)>P^+(n+3).
  \end{align*} The proof employs Selberg's upper-bound sieve and the multidimensional Selberg sieve developed in the Polymath8b work.

For positive integers $n,d$, let $A_d(n)$ denote the number of integers $0\le k\le n$ such that $\gcd\bigl(n,\binom nk\bigr)=d$, and put $S_d(x)=\sum_{n\le x}A_d(n)$. We prove that, for each fixed integer $d\ge2$, \[
  S_d(x)\sim\frac{d-1}{d}\,\mathfrak c\,\frac{x}{\log x}\qquad(x\to\infty),\qquad \mathfrak c=1.526453\ldots \] The key step is to extend the coprimality result of Ford and Konyagin to arbitrary fixed parameters $d\ge2$ and $1\le j<d$, obtaining \[
  \#\Bigl\{m\le x:\gcd\Bigl(m,\binom{dm}{jm}\Bigr)=1\Bigr\}
  \sim\mathfrak c\frac x{\log x}. \] The zero set of the corresponding Kummer carry function is a finite union of intervals of total measure $1/2$. We combine this property with estimates for small prime factors, Selberg trigonometric approximation, and exponential sum estimates over primes to count simultaneous carry conditions. We further prove that, for every fixed integer $e\ge1$, \[
  \#\Bigl\{m\le x:\gcd\Bigl(m,\binom{dm}{jm}\Bigr)=e\Bigr\}
  \sim\frac{\mathfrak c}{e}\frac x{\log x}. \]

We study empirical convergence rates for $(p,q)$-Gromov-Wasserstein (GW) distances $D_{p,q}$ for arbitrary real exponents $p>0$ and $q\ge1$. Let $\mu$ and $
u$ be probability measures on compact metric spaces $\mathcal{X}$ and $\mathcal{Y}$, and let $\widehat{\mu}_n$ and $\widehat{
u}_m$ be their empirical measures, constructed from independent i.i.d. samples of sizes $n$ and $m$. If the spaces $\mathcal{X},\,\mathcal{Y}$ are compact subsets of the Euclidean spaces $\mathbb{R}^{d_x}$ and $\mathbb{R}^{d_y}$, respectively, we obtain an upper bound of order $(n\wedge m)^{-(p\wedge2)/((d_x\wedge d_y)\vee 2(p\wedge2))}$ on the plug-in error of $D_{p,q}^p(\widehat{\mu}_n,\widehat{
u}_m)$ in expectation, up to a logarithmic factor at the critical dimension $d_x\wedge d_y=2(p\wedge2)$. On Euclidean balls, this rate is sharp up to the logarithmic factor. More generally, we show that the same upper bound holds when one space is a compact subset of a complete, connected $d$-dimensional Riemannian manifold and the other has polynomial covering numbers, with $d_x\wedge d_y$ replaced by $d$. Earlier works on $(2,2)$-GW, $(2,1)$-GW and other even-order cases rely on variational representations of GW distances that are specific to their exponents. We instead reduce the plug-in error to an analysis of empirical processes through local duality, which may be of independent interest.

In this note we solve a problem posed by Lagarias and Shallit concerning Lagrange constants under linear fractional transformations $Mx=\frac{ax+b}{cx+d}$. For an integer matrix $M$ with nonzero determinant and relatively prime entries, define the ratio spectrum \begin{equation*}
  \mathcal{V}(M)=\left\{\frac{k(Mx)}{k(x)}:x\in\mathrm{Bad}\right\}, \end{equation*} where $k(x)$ denotes the Lagrange constant of the irrational number $x$ and $\mathrm{Bad}$ is the set of badly approximable numbers. Lagarias and Shallit proved that \begin{equation*}
  \mathcal{V}(M)\subseteq\left[\frac{1}{|\det M|},|\det M|\right], \end{equation*} and asked for the determination of $\mathcal{V}(M)$. We prove that \begin{equation*}
  \mathcal{V}(M)=\left[\frac{1}{|\det M|},|\det M|\right]. \end{equation*}

Bilu and Linial conjectured that if $d\ge 2$, then every $d$-regular graph $G$ has an edge signing $\sigma:E(G)\to\{-1,1\}$ such that its signed adjacency matrix $A_\sigma$ satisfies the Ramanujan bound \begin{equation*}
  \rho(A_\sigma)\le 2\sqrt{d-1} \end{equation*} and they proved that \begin{equation*}
  \rho(A_\sigma)=O\!\left(\sqrt{d\log^3 d}\right). \end{equation*}
  Using a method of interlacing polynomials, Marcus, Spielman, and Srivastava confirmed one side of this conjecture that there is a signing $\sigma$ for which
  \[
  \lambda_{\max}(A_\sigma)\le 2\sqrt{d-1}. \] By constructing an auxiliary bipartite graph from a balanced orientation of $G$ and applying a bipartite signing argument, we prove that every finite simple graph $G$ of maximum degree $d\ge 3$ admits an edge signing $\sigma$ such that \[
  \rho(A_\sigma)
  \le 4\sqrt{\left\lceil \frac d2\right\rceil-1}
  \le 2\sqrt{2(d-1)}. \] This gives a new proof of Ravichandran and Srivastava's bound $2\sqrt{2(d-1)}$.

We formulate magnetostatics in $\mathbb{R}^3$ as a theory of stresses in which the vector potential $\mathbf{A}$ replaces the virtual velocity field of continuum mechanics. A current system is a collection of linear functionals on vector potentials, one for each subbody, whose values are interpreted as minus the potential energy. Cauchy's theorem gives a tensor field $\mathbf{\Sigma}$, the analog of the traction stress, such that the surface current on the boundary is $\mathbf{\Sigma} oldsymbol{
u}$. We assume only weak gauge invariance, that is, a gauge transformation $\mathbf{A}\mapsto\mathbf{A}+
abla\lambda$ changes the energy only through the values of $
abla\lambda$. This is the analog of the symmetry of the stress, and it holds if and only if $\mathbf{\Sigma}$ is skew-symmetric, so that it is represented by a vector field $\mathbf{M}$. The energy then takes the form $-\int(\mathbf{j}_f\cdot\mathbf{A}+\mathbf{M}\cdot\mathbf{B})\,dV$, which splits every current system uniquely into free current $\mathbf{j}_f$ and magnetization $\mathbf{M}$, with bound currents $
abla	imes\mathbf{M}$ and $\mathbf{M}	imes oldsymbol{
u}$. Gauge invariance of the energy is the analog of the balance of forces, and it singles out the current systems without free current. For currents embedded in matter, the force consists of the Lorentz force on the total current, an asymmetric active stress whose skew-symmetric part is the couple density $\mathbf{M}	imes\mathbf{B}$, and a gauge-dependent term that appears only where free current crosses the boundary of the subbody. This term is the rate at which the charge carriers transport potential momentum out of the subbody, so that the force on the matter itself is the gauge-invariant part.

An old conjecture dating back at least to Hildebrand's work in 1989, asserts that the M\"obius function exhibits cancellation on shifted primes; that is, $$\sum_{p\le x}\mu(p+2)=o(\pi(x)),\quad \text{or equivalently}\quad \sum_{n\le x}\Lambda(n)\mu(n+2)=o(x).$$ This is also a well-known model case for the parity problem in sieve theory. In this paper, we prove this conjecture. As an application, we show that each of the two sets $\{p:\, \mu(p+2)=1\}$ and $\{p:\, \mu(p+2)=-1\}$ has asymptotic density $1/2$ among the primes $p$ with $\mu^2(p+2)=1$: \begin{equation*}
  \lim_{x\to\infty}
  \frac{\#\{p\le x:\ \mu(p+2)=\kappa\}}
  {\#\{p\le x:\ \mu^2(p+2)=1\}}
  =\frac12,
  \quad \kappa\in\{-1,\,1\}. \end{equation*}
